 TION: Lokey Stem Cell Research Building \, G1161 Large Conference Room \n\
 nREGISTER HERE\n\nABOUT THE TALK \n\nStress is a major trigger of arrhythm
 ia\, yet the mechanisms underlying these effects remain poorly defined. Us
 ing a genetic model of ventricular tachycardia (VT)\, we find that natural
 istic emotional stressors rapidly provoke malignant arrhythmias in vulnera
 ble hearts. By tracing back from sympathetic neurons innervating myocardiu
 m\, we identify a multi-synaptic\, brain-to-heart circuit originating with
 in the midbrain\, which is required for stress-induced VT. Strikingly\, ac
 tivation of this pathway induces VT within seconds\, suggesting that centr
 al neuronal control of the heart is an important arrhythmogenic trigger. T
 ogether\, this work identifies a brain-to-heart circuit responsible for tr
 iggering stress-induced arrhythmias and points toward circuit-directed the
 rapies for this class of tachyarrhythmias. \n\nABOUT THE SPEAKER \n\nAlexa
 nder Nectow\, MD\, PhD\, is an Assistant Professor at Columbia University 
